 "answer": "I can't get a true live reading, but the most recent data I found shows **unhealthy to very unhealthy** air in Johor Bahru, which looks like a haze episode. The figures below are from around October 16, so they may not match conditions right now.\n\n**What the monitoring sites showed:**\n- **AQI.in (newest data):** AQI of 180, which is Unhealthy, with PM2.5 at 111 µg/m³ and PM10 at 151 µg/m³. Last updated 2026-10-16 at 15:51 local time.\n- **IQAir:** \"Johor Bahru Air Quality Index (AQI) is now Very unhealthy.\" PM2.5 was 30.7 times the World Health Organization's annual guideline. Its forecast had the AQI dropping from about 200 in the early afternoon to the 120s–140s by evening and the next day, and a daily average of 137 for \"today.\"\n- **AQICN:** 205, Very Unhealthy. This figure is measured by Malaysia's Department of Environment (DOE) and converted to the US EPA AQI standard.\n\nAll of these use the US AQI scale. Malaysia's own Air Pollutant Index (API) uses different numbers, so readings on local sites may not match.\n\n**If conditions are still like this:** limit time outdoors, wear an N95 mask if you go out, keep windows closed, and run an air purifier if you have one. This matters most for children, older people, and anyone with heart or lung problems.\n\n**For a current reading,** check the Malaysian government's APIMS site (eqms.doe.gov.my) or IQAir: https://www.iqair.com/air-quality/malaysia/johor/johor-bahru",
